#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:97
#. Diagram label
msgid "A change to who can do what"
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:129
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "A permission travels with the PARTICIPANT and cross-cuts every lane, so unlike a role it cannot be scoped by the diagram it is used in. Granting one is a standing decision, which is why this lane is person-only and why the log capture on this process is on."
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:48
#. Diagram label
msgid "Actor and role administration"
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:186
#. Diagram label
msgid "add actor"
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:110
#. Diagram label
msgid "Add an actor, and declare its kind"
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:69
#. Diagram label
msgid "Administrator"
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:166
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "AFTER the change, not before. Every criterion here is a join — role to lane, actor to role, activity to skill — so what an administrative edit breaks is not in the file it edited. The sidecars under `test/results/kg-qa/` are committed for the reason a printed verdict is not: without them, \"unbound since it was drawn\" and \"broken by this change\" are indistinguishable."
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:70
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "All four administration verbs — add, assign, grant, retire — converge at Gateway_Changed onto the same CallActivity_CodeChangeReview, so no kind of substrate edit gets a lighter review path than another; and Gateway_AuditClean's 'no' branch (A13) loops back to this lane rather than ending the process, because whether a `major` finding holds the change is this lane's policy call, not something Task_RunKgAudit decides for it."
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:138
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "An actor id is referenced from roles, diagrams, beans and commits, so removing the file leaves every one of those unable to tell retirement from accident. Same rule, and the same reason, as a scrapped bean."
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:187
#. Diagram label
msgid "assign roles"
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:174
#. Diagram label
msgid "Audit clean?"
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:165
#. Diagram label
msgid "Audit the graph [kg:audit]"
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:155
#. Diagram label
msgid "Branch, gates, PR and review [code-change-review]"
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:156
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Called rather than restated. The mechanics of getting an edit reviewed and merged are not specific to the role graph, and a second description of them is a second thing free to disagree with the first."
msgstr ""

#: processes/actor-role-administration.bpmn:52
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Changing who can do what: adding an actor, opening or closing a role to one, granting or revoking a permission — and auditing the graph afterwards, because the declaration and the graph are two things that can disagree. You are in this process when the substrate every other diagram's lanes bind to is what is changing. That is why it is drawn rather than left as a filesystem edit: a role added without an audit is a lane binding that resolves today and may not tomorrow, and nothing else in the corpus would notice. Retirement, never deletion. An actor id is referenced from commits, sidecars and other declarations, so removing one makes an old record unreadable while retiring one leaves it legible and says the actor has stopped."
msgstr ""

